Sure! Those numbers are already a pair of latitude/longitude coordinates:

| Latitude | Longitude |
|----------|-----------|
| 37.4224764 | –122.0842499 |

If you feed them into the Google Maps Geocoding API (or any reverse‑geocoding service) you’ll get back the human‑readable address for that spot. Here’s what a typical request and response would look like.

---

### 1️⃣ Request

```http
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

> **Note**  
> Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual Google Maps API key.  
> If you’re using a different provider (e.g., OpenStreetMap Nominatim), the URL will differ but the idea is the same: supply the `latlng` parameter.

---

